<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Okubo Toshimichi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1830–1878</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ōkubo Toshimichi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Okubo Toshimichi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a> and one of the leading statesmen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigo Takamori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kido-takayoshi">Kido Takayoshi</a> he engineered the alliance of Satsuma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> and the overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, then directed the new government's reforms, including the abolition of the domains and a new land tax. He opposed an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a> and was assassinated in Tokyo in 1878.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi.jpg" alt="Okubo Toshimichi" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 明治5年2月、ワシントンDCで撮影。大蔵卿時代。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%8Ckubo_Toshimichi_1872_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
